       CT angio head and neck stroke protocol as a code stroke was called which demonstrated:

      1\. Large remote left MCA territory ischemic infarct with cystic encephalomalacia and marginal gliosis. There is attenuation of the left MCA branch vessels in this region with nonvisualization of the inferior division M2 branches and narrowing and nonvisualization of distal opercular and M3 to 4 branches. These findings are likely chronic in nature. Diffusion-weighted MRI is recommended to exclude the possibility of superimposed new or acute ischemic infarction. &#x20;

       2\. The remaining intracranial vessels are patent and normal in caliber without intracranial aneurysm or vascular malformation. No other evidence of large branch occlusion. &#x20;

       3\. Fibrocalcific plaque at the origin of the left internal carotid artery without hemodynamically significant stenosis. The remaining neck vessels are patent and normal in caliber without hemodynamically significant stenosis or dissection. &#x20;



       CT head demonstrating:

      1\. Moderate diffuse atrophy with extensive periventricular changes suggesting chronic small-vessel ischemic disease. &#x20;

       2\. More prominent, focal areas of ill-defined decreased density area encephalomalacia involving the left anterior temporal to frontal region as well as the posterior right temporal lobe. &#x20;

       3\. No definite acute lesion noted. &#x20;

        &#x20;

